    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-89040",
      "knowledge_point_id": "f0f7055d-9f45-5115-97f4-35d0c56008e0",
      "knowledge_point": "Using The Law of Total Probability Stated in Terms of Conditional Probability",
      "problem": "In a gas station, $42%$ of the clients buy regular gasoline while $58%$ buy premium gasoline. If $62%$ of the clients that buy regular gasoline paid with a credit card while $55%$ of the clients that buy premium gasoline paid with a credit card, what is the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "Let $C$ be the event that the chosen person paid with a credit card, and let $R$ be the event that the person bought regular gasoline. Then ${R}^{′}$ is the event that the person bought premium gasoline.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected person paid with a credit card, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(C)=P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|{R}^{′})P({R}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $42%$ of the clients buy regular gasoline while $58%$ buy premium gasoline, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.42,\\,P({R}^{′})=0.58.\n$$\n\n- Since $62%$ of the clients that buy regular gasoline paid with a credit card while $55%$ of the clients that buy premium gasoline paid with a credit card, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|R)=0.62,\\,P(C|{R}^{′})=0.55.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|{R}^{′})P({R}^{′}) \\\\  & =(0.62)(0.42)+(0.55)(0.58) \\\\  & ≈0.579\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.",
