  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948571",
    "id": "q-89027",
    "status": "supported",
    "reason": null,
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-89027",
      "knowledge_point_id": "f0f7055d-9f45-5115-97f4-35d0c56008e0",
      "knowledge_point": "Using The Law of Total Probability Stated in Terms of Conditional Probability",
      "problem": "John keeps his marbles in two boxes, box $A$ and box $B.$ Of the marbles in his collection, $\\frac{3}{4}$ of them are in box $A,$ and $\\frac{1}{4}$ of them are in box $B.$ If $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the marbles in box $A$ are red and $\\frac{3}{5}$ of the marbles in box $B$ are red, what is the probability that a randomly selected marble in John's collection is red?",
      "worked_solution": "Let $R$ be the event that the chosen marble is red, and let $A$ be the event that the marble was selected from box $A.$ Then, ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the marble was selected from box $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected marble is red, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(R)=P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $\\frac{3}{4}$ of the marbles are in box $A$ and $\\frac{1}{4}$ are in box $B,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=\\frac{3}{4},\\,P({A}^{′})=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the marbles in box $A$ are red and $\\frac{3}{5}$ of the marbles in box $B$ are red, we have\n\n$$\nP(R|A)=\\frac{1}{3},\\,P(R|{A}^{′})=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected marble is red is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3})(\\frac{3}{4})+(\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{5+3}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
