      "knowledge_point": "Solving for an Unknown Using the Law of Total Probability",
      "problem": "Jacob has two email accounts, account $A$ and account $B.$ In account $A$ he receives $40%$ of his emails and he receives the remaining $60%$ in account $B.$ Approximately $30%$ of the emails received in account $A$ are spam and $36%$ of the emails received in both accounts are spam. What is the probability that a randomly selected email from account $B$ is spam? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "Let $S$ be the event that the chosen email is spam, and let $A$ be the event that the email selected is from account $A.$ Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the email selected is from account $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected email is spam, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(S)=P(S|A)P(A)+P(S|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $40%$ of the emails are received in account $A$ and the remaining $60%$ in account $B,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.40,\\,P({A}^{′})=0.60.\n$$\n\n- Since $30%$ of the emails received in account $A$ are spam, we have \n\n$$\nP(S|A)=0.30.\n$$\n\n- Since $36%$ of the emails received in both accounts are spam, we have \n\n$$\nP(S)=0.36.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(S|{A}^{′})$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S|A)P(A)+P(S|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.36 & =(0.30)(0.40)+P(S|{A}^{′})(0.60) \\\\ 0.36 & =0.12+0.6P(S|{A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.6P(S|{A}^{′}) & =0.24 \\\\ P(S|{A}^{′}) & =0.40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected email from account $B$ is spam is $0.40.$",
